# Database schema: no unilateral wipe or apply

## Never do these unless the user explicitly asks

- Run, execute, or pipe [`schema/dropAll.sql`](schema/dropAll.sql) (or any equivalent “drop all tables” / full schema wipe).
- Drop, truncate, or recreate the local or remote database to “fix” schema drift.
- Re-run [`schema/setup.sql`](schema/setup.sql) against a non-empty database as an upgrade path.
- Run `npm run migrate` (or otherwise apply migrations) against any database without asking first.

## Always ask first

Before any destructive or environment-mutating DDL (drops, wipes, migrate against a shared/prod/local DB the user owns), **stop and get explicit confirmation**. “Implement the migration file” is not permission to apply it.

## Normal agent workflow

1. Update [`schema/setup.sql`](schema/setup.sql) **and** add an idempotent file under [`schema/migrations/`](schema/migrations/) in the same change (setup = new DBs; migration = existing DBs). Never require “setup then migrate” for one install.
2. Document the change; leave applying SQL to the user (`npm run migrate` on existing DBs, or DataGrip / another DB IDE).

## If schema appears wrong locally

Do **not** assume wipe + recreate. Tell the user what looks wrong and ask how they want to proceed (they usually migrate or apply SQL themselves).
